{
 "id": "M22",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "A handful of these backdoors alarm Opus and nothing else",
 "section": "results",
 "order": 135,
 "caption": "One row per hard backdoor, 51 of them, ordered by Opus 5.5's score at xhigh effort. All three monitors score 0 to 100. Opus clears 50 on 13 rows where Sonnet and Haiku never go above 30. Solid Opus dots are backdoors no other monitor caught; hollow ones, at least one did.",
 "notes": [
  "Hollow means at least one of our seven monitors caught it in some version of the run (on the 0 to 100 scale, or one Haiku 0 to 10 run). That's why it's in this hard set but not among the 41 nothing caught.",
  "The dashed yellow line is 50 out of 100. Opus has no honest baseline here, so it has no cutoff of its own and no audit budget: that line is a reading aid, not an operating point, and a real deployment would set one against honest code instead.",
  "The ordering is Opus's own, so the chart cannot help but look favourable to Opus at the top. The comparison that matters is horizontal, within a row, where Sonnet and Haiku had the same code in front of them.",
  "Sonnet's and Haiku's medians on this set are 1 and 2 out of 100, which is what being a hard backdoor means: these are the programs our monitors found boring.",
  "Thin data. 51 rows is the whole hard set, and the never-caught group inside it is 41. Single scores, one pass each, no repeat draws, so a row's height is one measurement."
 ],
